<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.7.3: Implementing General Strategies for Factoring</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01701</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.7.3: Implementing General Strategies for Factoring</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d9f86ea5-c785-4b6a-9a1f-7e172bc88ef8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Activity</title>
<para id="para-00001">Work with a partner on these problems. After each solution, discuss if there are multiple strategies that would work to factor the polynomial.</para>
<para id="para-00002">Find a strategy that fits and factor completely:</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    <para id="para-00003">\(5y^3 − 15y^2 − 270y\)</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
    
    <para id="para-00004">You can use the following video if you need some help.</para>
    
    <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/143008d05c5725e4e42c282ce15ca1124926fe73"/></media>
  
  <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00006">Factor out GCF of \(5y\). Then use the “undo FOIL” method on the polynomial of the form \(x^2 + bx +c\).
      \(5y(y − 9)(y + 6)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00002">
  <problem id="prob-00002">
    <para id="para-00007">\(75m^3 + 12m\)</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00002">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00009">Factor out GCF of \(3m\).
      \(3m(25m^2 + 4)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00003">
  <problem id="prob-00003">
    <para id="para-00010">\(16x^2 + 24xy − 4x − 6y\)</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00003">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00012">Factor out GCF of \(2\). Then use the grouping method.
      \(2(4x − 1)(2x + 3y)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00004">
  <problem id="prob-00004">
    <para id="para-00013">\(2n^2 + 13n − 7\)</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00004">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00015">Use the “\(ac\)” method or trial and error.
      \((2n − 1)(n + 7)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00005">
  <problem id="prob-00005">
    <para id="para-00016">\(121r^2 − s^2\)</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00005">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00018">Use the difference of squares method.
      \((11r − s)(11r + s)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00006">
  <problem id="prob-00006">
    <para id="para-00019">\(25w^2 − 60w + 36\)</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00006">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00021">Use the perfect square trinomial method.
      \((5w − 6)^2\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00007">
  <problem id="prob-00007">
    <para id="para-00022">\((3x + 1)^2 − 6(3x + 1) + 9\)</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00007">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00023">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00024">Use the substitution or perfect square trinomial method.
      \((3x−2)^2\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00008">
  <problem id="prob-00008">
    <para id="para-00025">\(16x^2 − 24xy + 9y^2 − 64\)</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00008">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00026">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00027">Use the grouping method on the first three terms. They are a perfect square trinomial. Then use the difference of squares on the result.
      \((4x − 3y − 8)(4x − 3y + 8)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

</section>
<section id="sect-00002">
<title>Video: Determining Factoring Method</title>
<para id="para-00028">Watch the following video to learn more about how to determine which method of factoring will be best to apply.</para>
<media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9381e45e777a0dff0ef45e7c94cae95307904b38"/></media></section>
<note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
  (    <para id="para-00029">Which strategy would you use to factor \(9x^2 − 24xy + 16y^2\)? What is the factored result?</para>)
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
  <section id="sect-00004"><title>Implementing General Strategies for Factoring</title>
    <para id="para-00030">Let’s practice recognizing and implementing these strategies as we factor polynomials.</para>
    <para id="para-00031">Remember, a polynomial is completely factored if, other than monomials, its factors are prime!</para>
    <para id="para-00032"><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
    <para id="para-00033">Find a strategy that fits and factor completely: \(7x^3 − 21x^2 − 70x\).</para>
    <para id="para-00034"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Is there a GCF?
      \(7x^3 − 21x^2 − 70x\)
      Yes, \(7x\).</para>
    <para id="para-00035"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Factor out the GCF.
      \(7x(x^2 − 3x − 10)\)</para>
    <para id="para-00036"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - In the parentheses, is it a binomial, a trinomial, or are there more than three terms?
      Trinomial with leading coefficient \(1\).</para>
    <para id="para-00037"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - “Undo” FOIL.
      \(7x(x \;\;\;)(x \;\;\; )\)
      \(7x(x + 2)(x − 5)\)</para>
    <para id="para-00038"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Is the expression factored completely? 
      Yes. Neither binomial can be factored.</para>
    <para id="para-00039"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Check your answer by multiplying.
      \(7x(x + 2)(x − 5)\)
      \(7x(x^2 − 5x + 2x − 10)\)
      \(7x(x^2 − 3x − 10)\)
      \(7x^3 − 21x^2 − 70x&amp;checkmark;\)</para>
    <para id="para-00040"><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
    <para id="para-00041">Find a strategy that fits and factor completely: \(4x^2 + 8bx − 4ax − 8ab\).</para>
    <para id="para-00042"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Is there a GCF?
      \(4x^2 + 8bx − 4ax − 8ab\)
      Factor out the GCF, \(4\).
      \(4(x^2 + 2bx − ax − 2ab)\)</para>
    <para id="para-00043"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - There are four terms. Use grouping.
      \(4 \lbrack x(x + 2b) − a(x + 2b) \rbrack\)
      \(4(x + 2b)(x − a)\)</para>
    <para id="para-00044"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Is the expression factored completely?
      Yes.</para>
    <para id="para-00045"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Check your answer by multiplying. 
      \(4(x + 2b)(x − a)\) 
      \(4(x^2 − ax + 2bx − 2ab)\) 
      \(4x^2 + 8bx − 4ax − 8ab&amp;checkmark;\)</para>
    <para id="para-00046"><emphasis>Example 3</emphasis></para>
    <para id="para-00047">Find a strategy that fits and factor completely: \(40x^2y + 44xy − 24y\).</para>
    <para id="para-00048"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Is there a GCF? 
      \(40x^2y + 44xy − 24y\) 
      Factor out the GCF, \(4y\). 
      \(4y(10x^2 + 11x − 6)\)</para>
    <para id="para-00049"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Factor the trinomial with \(a \neq 1\) using the “\(ac\)” method or trial and error.
      \(4y(5x − 2)(2x + 3)\)</para>
    <para id="para-00050"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Is the expression factored completely? 
      Yes.</para>
    <para id="para-00051"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Check your answer by multiplying. 
      \(4y(5x − 2)(2x + 3)\) 
      \(4y(10x^2 + 11x − 6)\) 
      \(40x^2y + 44xy − 24y&amp;checkmark;\)</para>
    <note class="try" id="note-00002">
    <title>Try It: Implementing General Strategies for Factoring</title>
    
    <exercise id="exer-00009">
      <problem id="prob-00009">
        
        <para id="para-00052">Find a strategy that fits and factor completely:</para>
        
        <para id="para-00053">\(27y^2 − 48\)</para>
        <para id="para-00054">\(6x^2 − 12xc + 6bx − 12bc\).</para>
        <para id="para-00055">Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
      
      </problem><solution id="sol-00009">
      <para id="para-00056">Here is how to factor these polynomials:</para>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>difference of squares; \(3(3y − 4)(3y + 4)\)</item>
      </list>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
        <item>grouping; \(6(x + b)(x − 2c)\)</item>
      </list>
    
    </solution></exercise></note>
    </section>
    </section>
    
